  "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\cdot 0-2y) \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,\\sqrt{\\pi })} & =4(\\sqrt{\\pi })\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
